00:00:03.350 --> 00:00:05.910 align:start position:0%
in australia scientists have burned  10 pig carcasses in an unprecedented  experiment  they're trying to answer a question from  the other side of the globe  what happened to these 43 students who  disappeared in mexico  two years ago the mexican government  maintains that the students from a  teacher's college  were kidnapped by a drug cartel they say  their bodies were incinerated atop a  pile of wood and tires  in a trash dump in the town of cucula  several bags of human remains burned to  ash  were found in the dump and in a nearby  river  last summer fire scientist jose carrero  went to kakula  to investigate the government's story he  saw no signs of a massive fire  what's more there was virtually no  organic material  left in their remains that suggests they  were burned  in a crematorium not an open-air fire  he decided to do some experiments to be  sure working with pig carcasses  torero used wood fuel to burn anywhere  from one  to four pigs at a time he found that  over 700 kilograms of wood was needed  to burn one pig carcass to the point  where there was almost no  organic matter left burning more than  one body at a time  required even more fuel per body  according to these results  burning 43 bodies would require over 30  000 kilograms of wood this suggests that  the students bodies were likely  not burned in the dump their final hours  are still unaccounted for  with the dump site ruled out it seems  investigators should continue to look  elsewhere  you
 

